{
  "esm": "1.2.0",
  "metadata": {
    "name": "MosaicCoagulationAssembly",
    "description": "MOSAIC's coagulation as a RATE: the Jacobson semi-implicit sectional scheme's bin-splitting rule and the assembly of the pair kernel into each bin's number and composition tendencies -- SUBROUTINE coagsolv of chem/module_mosaic_coag.F (WRF v4.8.0), loops 30-40 (the splitting tables) and the production and loss sums of loops 465-475, evaluated at the input distribution (Jacobson et al. 1994; Jacobson 2005, section 15.2).\n\nTHE SPLITTING RULE. A collision of a particle from bin i with one from bin j makes a particle of dry volume v_i + v_j, where v_k = (4 pi / 3)(dp_k / 2)^3 is the dry volume per particle of bin k at its current dry diameter dp_k. If v_i + v_j is at or above the largest bin's volume the whole product goes to the largest bin. Otherwise it lies in [v_kk, v_(kk+1)) for exactly one kk -- the number of bins whose volume is at or below it, which is lib/table_interp.esm's bracket_index on the volume axis -- and is split between the two bracketing bins so that VOLUME and NUMBER are both conserved: the fraction f_lo = ((v_(kk+1) - v_i - v_j)/(v_(kk+1) - v_kk)) v_kk/(v_i + v_j) of the colliding volume goes to bin kk and 1 - f_lo to bin kk+1. fink(i, j, k) is that fraction for bin k. Two asymmetries, both coagsolv's: the low share is counted as PRODUCTION into kk only when the partner j is smaller than kk (j < kk); when j = kk it is not a gain for kk at all, and correspondingly the LOSS fraction of bin i in an i+j collision, floss(i, j), is 1 if i < kk, the high share 1 - f_lo if i = kk (the part that stays in i is no loss), 0 if i > kk, and in the top-lump case 1 unless i is itself the largest bin, which never loses by self-coagulation. These are written here as the closed-form table, not as coagsolv's sparse (jbins, jbinik) bookkeeping, and pinned ENTRY BY ENTRY against the dense table the instrumented Fortran scatters out, so a wrong entry cannot hide behind a compensating one in the assembled rate.\n\nTHE RATES. With N_k the number concentration and q_k any one composition quantity carried by bin k (a component's mass or volume mixing ratio, aerosol water, the dry volume -- the operator is linear and identical for each, so a consumer maps this component over its components):\n  dN_k/dt = (1/v_k) sum_(i,j) fink(i,j,k) K_ij N_i N_j v_j  -  N_k sum_j floss(k,j) K_kj N_j\n  dq_k/dt = sum_(i,j) fink(i,j,k) K_ij N_i q_j  -  q_k sum_j floss(k,j) K_kj N_j\nwith K_ij the Brownian pair kernel of coagulation_kernel.esm (supplied here as the matrix cbr). The sums are the sectional Smoluchowski integrals and are written as sums over the bin pairs. coagsolv's guards `k > 1` on production and `k < nbin` on loss are not written: the splitting rule already makes both terms vanish there (no product lands in bin 1, and the largest bin's floss row is identically zero), and the tests pin both end bins.\n\nWHY A RATE AND NOT coagsolv'S UPDATE. coagsolv advances the state with the semi-implicit step c_new = (c + prod dt)/(1 + loss dt) over one sub-step of the chemistry step, and the resulting real32 increment is unresolvable for a large fraction of entries (EqWeFiC FORTRAN_BUGS N87). The instrumented model records the instantaneous rate at the input distribution where it forms (rate_coag_num, rate_coag_vol); that is the d/dt of the scheme and what this component reproduces. The semi-implicit step is the Fortran's integrator, not its physics.\n\nNOT HERE: mosaic_coag_1clm's preparation of the distribution from the MOSAIC state (dry mass, volume and density, the number-conforming clamp into the section's volume bounds, the wet diameter and density the kernel uses) -- the dry diameters and the distribution are inputs, recorded at coagsolv's entry.\n\nAXES (2026-11-13). The operator runs on every cell of a column at once: lev (NLEV, default 1) leads every array, and the composition quantity q carries a trailing axis cq (NQ, default 1) so that one mount maps every column coagsolv carries (twelve in couplings/mosaic_aerchemistry_column.esm). The bracket on the per-cell section-volume axis is lib/table_interp.esm's bracket_index_in_row. With NLEV = NQ = 1 the tests are the single-cell ones, unchanged (156/156).",
    "authors": [
      "EqWeFiC"
    ],
    "created": "2026-11-12T00:00:00Z",
    "tags": [
      "mosaic",
      "aerosol",
      "coagulation",
      "sectional",
      "wrf-chem",
      "chem_opt-170"
    ],
    "references": [
      {
        "citation": "Jacobson, M. Z., Turco, R. P., Jensen, E. J. and Toon, O. B. (1994). Modeling coagulation among particles of different composition and size. Atmos. Environ. 28A, 1327-1338."
      },
      {
        "citation": "Jacobson, M. Z. (2005). Fundamentals of Atmospheric Modeling, 2nd ed., Cambridge University Press, section 15.2."
      },
      {
        "citation": "Zaveri, R. A., Easter, R. C., Fast, J. D. and Peters, L. K. (2008). Model for Simulating Aerosol Interactions and Chemistry (MOSAIC). J. Geophys. Res. 113, D13204."
      }
    ]
  },
  "metaparameters": {
    "NLEV": {
      "type": "integer",
      "default": 1,
      "description": "Grid cells (model levels) evaluated at once; 1 for a single cell. Bound by a column coupling."
    },
    "NBIN": {
      "type": "integer",
      "default": 8,
      "description": "Number of MOSAIC size sections (8 for chem_opt = 170)."
    },
    "NQ": {
      "type": "integer",
      "default": 1,
      "description": "Composition quantities carried through the operator at once (coagsolv's distrib columns 2..; 13 in chem_opt = 170: ten components, hysteresis water, water and dry volume). 1 maps one quantity, as a box test does."
    }
  },
  "index_sets": {
    "lev": {
      "kind": "interval",
      "size": "NLEV"
    },
    "bin": {
      "kind": "interval",
      "size": "NBIN"
    },
    "cq": {
      "kind": "interval",
      "size": "NQ"
    }
  },
  "models": {
    "MosaicCoagulationAssembly": {
      "subsystems": {
        "par": {
          "ref": "./mosaic_coagulation_parameters.esm"
        }
      },
      "expression_template_imports": [
        {
          "ref": "../../../lib/table_interp.esm"
        }
      ],
      "variables": {
        "dp_dry": {
          "type": "parameter",
          "units": "cm",
          "shape": [
            "lev",
            "bin"
          ],
          "default": 1e-05,
          "description": "Dry diameter of each bin at its current mean size (coagsolv dpdry_inp), cm."
        },
        "cbr": {
          "type": "parameter",
          "units": "cm^3/s",
          "shape": [
            "lev",
            "bin",
            "bin"
          ],
          "default": 1e-09,
          "description": "Brownian coagulation kernel of each bin pair (coagulation_kernel.esm), cm3 per particle per second."
        },
        "num": {
          "type": "parameter",
          "units": "cm^-3",
          "shape": [
            "lev",
            "bin"
          ],
          "default": 1000.0,
          "description": "Number concentration of each bin (coagsolv distrib(:,1)), particles per cm3 of air."
        },
        "q": {
          "type": "parameter",
          "units": "1",
          "shape": [
            "lev",
            "bin",
            "cq"
          ],
          "default": 1e-12,
          "description": "One composition quantity carried by each bin (coagsolv distrib(:,jaer>1)): a component's mixing ratio, aerosol water, or the dry volume. The operator is linear and unit-agnostic in q; the tendency carries q's unit per second."
        },
        "vpart": {
          "type": "unknown",
          "units": "cm^3",
          "shape": [
            "lev",
            "bin"
          ],
          "description": "Dry volume per particle of each bin, (4 pi / 3)(dp_dry/2)^3 (coagsolv volume(j))."
        },
        "vsum": {
          "type": "unknown",
          "units": "cm^3",
          "shape": [
            "lev",
            "bin",
            "bin"
          ],
          "description": "Dry volume of the product of an i+j collision."
        },
        "kk": {
          "type": "unknown",
          "units": "1",
          "shape": [
            "lev",
            "bin",
            "bin"
          ],
          "description": "Lower bracketing bin of each collision product on the bin-volume axis (the number of bins at or below it, held to [1, NBIN-1])."
        },
        "f_lo": {
          "type": "unknown",
          "units": "1",
          "shape": [
            "lev",
            "bin",
            "bin"
          ],
          "description": "Fraction of the colliding volume assigned to the lower bracketing bin kk (coagsolv finklow); meaningful only when the product is below the largest bin."
        },
        "fink": {
          "type": "unknown",
          "units": "1",
          "shape": [
            "lev",
            "bin",
            "bin",
            "bin"
          ],
          "description": "Volume fraction of an i+j collision counted as production into bin k (coagsolv fink, dense)."
        },
        "floss": {
          "type": "unknown",
          "units": "1",
          "shape": [
            "lev",
            "bin",
            "bin"
          ],
          "description": "Fraction of bin i lost per i+j collision (coagsolv floss)."
        },
        "loss_freq": {
          "type": "unknown",
          "units": "1/s",
          "shape": [
            "lev",
            "bin"
          ],
          "description": "First-order coagulational loss frequency of bin k, sum_j floss(k,j) K_kj N_j (coagsolv aloss/deltat)."
        },
        "dnum_dt": {
          "type": "unknown",
          "units": "cm^-3/s",
          "shape": [
            "lev",
            "bin"
          ],
          "description": "Instantaneous coagulation tendency of each bin's number concentration (instrumented rate_coag_num)."
        },
        "dq_dt": {
          "type": "unknown",
          "units": "1/s",
          "shape": [
            "lev",
            "bin",
            "cq"
          ],
          "description": "Instantaneous coagulation tendency of the composition quantity q in each bin (instrumented rate_coag_vol for that component)."
        }
      },
